.TH CLAUDEME 1 "August 2026" "claudeme 0.0.8" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
claudeme \- Claude Code account switcher
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claudeme
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B claudeme
is a terminal application for managing and switching between multiple Claude Code
accounts (e.g., personal pro and company teams). Each account gets its own
\fBCLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR\fR holding its authentication; sessions, projects and settings
are shared between accounts by symlink. A shell hook exports the environment
variable so \fBclaude\fR uses the right account.
.SH HOW IT WORKS
Each account is stored in its own directory at
\fB~/.config/claudeme/accounts/<email>/\fR.
When you run \fBclaudeme add\fR, Claude Code is launched with
\fBCLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR\fR pointing to a staging directory. After you authenticate and
exit, the directory is renamed to match the account email.
.PP
Switching accounts sets the active profile in \fBprofiles.json\fR. The shell hook
exports \fBCLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR\fR to point to the active profile's directory. No
file swapping or keychain manipulation is needed.
.PP
The active profile is global, so every terminal follows it. To run two accounts at
once, override it per launch with \fBclaudeme as\fR or per terminal with
\fBclaudeme pin\fR, which sets \fBCLAUDEME_PROFILE\fR in that shell only.
.PP
Short aliases can be assigned to accounts (e.g., \fBwork\fR for
\fBuser@company.com\fR) and used with any command that accepts an account
reference.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Account Management
.TP
.B claudeme \fR[\fICLAUDE-ARGS\fR...]
Launch \fBclaude\fR with the resolved profile. Unrecognised arguments are
forwarded to \fBclaude\fR.
.TP
.B claudeme tui
Launch the interactive TUI to select or delete an account.
.TP
.B claudeme add
Add a new account. Launches \fBclaude\fR for authentication, then registers the
account by email.
.TP
.B claudeme list\fR, \fBclaudeme ls
List all accounts with active marker and aliases.
.TP
.B claudeme use \fIALIAS\fR|\fIEMAIL
Switch the active account, and remember it for the current directory.
.TP
.B claudeme as \fIALIAS\fR|\fIEMAIL\fR [\fICLAUDE-ARGS\fR...]
Launch \fBclaude\fR with that account without changing the active one, so two
terminals can run two accounts in parallel.
.TP
.B claudeme pin \fIALIAS\fR|\fIEMAIL\fR, \fBclaudeme unpin
Pin the current shell to one account by setting \fBCLAUDEME_PROFILE\fR. A pinned
shell overrides the active profile and skips auto\-switching. Requires the shell
hook (\fBeval "$(claudeme hook)"\fR).
.TP
.B claudeme me\fR, \fBclaudeme whoami
Print the active profile directory.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dir
Output only the profile directory path (used by shell hook).
.TP
.B \-\-short
Output alias or email (used by status line).
.RE
.TP
.B claudeme remove \fIALIAS\fR|\fIEMAIL\fR, \fBclaudeme rm
Remove an account and its config directory.
.TP
.B claudeme reset
Remove all accounts, aliases, and config. Start fresh.
.TP
.B claudeme resolve \fIALIAS\fR|\fIEMAIL
Print the email an alias points at, or exit non\-zero if no such account exists.
Used by the shell hook to validate \fBpin\fR.
.TP
.B claudeme sessions\fR, \fBclaudeme ss
List recent sessions for the current project.
.TP
.B claudeme version\fR, \fBclaudeme \-v\fR, \fBclaudeme \-\-version
Show version, commit, and build date.
.SS Aliases
.TP
.B claudeme alias add \fINAME\fR \fIEMAIL
Create an alias for an account email.
.TP
.B claudeme alias list\fR, \fBclaudeme alias ls
List all aliases.
.TP
.B claudeme alias rm \fINAME
Remove an alias.
.SS Auto-switch
.TP
.B claudeme auto
Check rules for the current directory and output an \fBexport\fR command to
stdout if a match is found (designed for shell hook eval).
.TP
.B claudeme rule add \fIPATTERN\fR \fIALIAS\fR|\fIEMAIL
Add an auto-switch rule mapping a directory pattern to an account.
.TP
.B claudeme rule list\fR, \fBclaudeme rule ls
List all auto-switch rules.
.TP
.B claudeme rule rm \fIPATTERN
Remove an auto-switch rule.
.TP
.B claudeme folder list
List remembered directory\-to\-account mappings.
.TP
.B claudeme folder set \fIALIAS\fR|\fIEMAIL
Map the current directory to an account. Takes priority over rules.
.TP
.B claudeme folder rm \fR[\fIPATH\fR]
Remove the mapping for \fIPATH\fR, or for the current directory.
.TP
.B claudeme folder clear
Remove all folder mappings.
.TP
.B claudeme config auto_apply on\fR|\fBoff
Toggle whether matching rules auto-switch or just warn. A pinned shell is exempt
either way.
.TP
.B claudeme hook
Print the shell hook for \fB.zshrc\fR or \fB.bashrc\fR.
Add \fBeval "$(claudeme hook)"\fR to your shell rc file.
.TP
.B claudeme help\fR, \fBclaudeme \-\-help\fR, \fBclaudeme \-h
Show help information.
.SS Usage reports
.TP
.B claudeme projects \fR[\fB\-\-cost\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fIQUERY\fR]
List the projects found in the shared transcript directory. Transcript folders
are clustered by working directory, so subdirectories, prototypes and worktree
copies fold into the checkout they belong to.
\fB\-\-cost\fR adds a cost column, which scans every transcript, and prints each
project's spend per day and per skill beneath its row. Both are summed from the
working directories the project owns, so they add up to its cost column.
\fB\-\-all\fR includes transcript folders that hold no sessions.
.TP
.B claudeme usage \fR[\fIQUERY\fR] [\fB\-\-top\fR \fIN\fR]
Report token spend for one project, or for all of them when \fIQUERY\fR is
omitted. An exact project name wins; otherwise \fIQUERY\fR matches any project
whose name or path contains it. Breaks spend down by day, model, skill,
session, main loop vs subagent, and agent type. \fB\-\-top\fR limits the session
table
(default 20).
.TP
.B claudeme heatmap \fR[\fIQUERY\fR] [\fB\-\-weeks\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-metric\fR \fIM\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-live\fR]
Draw activity as a grid of days, one square per day, weeks running left to
right — the GitHub contribution graph for Claude Code. Shades are quartiles of
the non-zero days on screen, so a quiet window still shows its own busy days.
\fB\-\-metric\fR selects \fBcost\fR (default), \fBcalls\fR or \fBsessions\fR.
\fB\-\-weeks\fR sets the width; the default is as much of a year as the terminal
fits, and \fB\-\-all\fR reaches back to the first day with any activity.
Days come from the snapshot history and the transcripts on disk, taking the
larger of the two per day, so the grid spans further back than retention;
\fB\-\-live\fR reads the transcripts alone. A \fIQUERY\fR scopes the grid to one
project, matched as in \fBusage\fR; per-project session counts were never
recorded in history, so \fB\-\-metric sessions\fR with a \fIQUERY\fR covers only
the transcripts still on disk.
.TP
.B claudeme digest \fR[\fB\-\-since\fR \fIDATE\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-archived\fR] [\fB\-\-metrics\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-tokens\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-install\fR] [\fB\-\-uninstall\fR] [\fB\-\-status\fR]
Summarize session transcripts into
.IR ~/.config/claudeme/shared/history/<date>/<project>.json .
Each session becomes one structured record — goal, outcome, work items,
decisions, commands of note, dead ends, next\-session brief — written by
\fBgpt\-5.6\-luna\fR through \fBcodex exec\fR against a fixed schema. Transcripts
are only ever read; Claude Code owns their lifetime, and the digest is what
survives its \fBcleanupPeriodDays\fR cleanup.
Records are keyed by session id, so a session is never summarized twice and an
interrupted run resumes where it stopped.
The default window is yesterday; \fB\-\-since\fR widens it, \fB\-\-all\fR covers
every session on disk, \fB\-\-archived\fR covers only the gzipped ones under
.IR ~/.config/claudeme/shared/archive ,
and \fB\-\-limit\fR stops after \fIN\fR sessions.
\fB\-\-metrics\-only\fR skips the model entirely and records just the
deterministic half — when the session ran, on which branches, and the counted
totals from \fBdistill.py\fR. It covers every date, adds the \fBmetrics\fR field
to records summarized before it existed, and leaves an existing summary
untouched; a session it records is still owed a summary by a later run.
\fB\-\-tokens\-only\fR likewise skips the model and records only the token
ledger: calls, input, output and cache tokens per model and per day, stored
unvalued so \fBclaudeme cost\fR can price them against any date's table.
Both flags merge onto whatever record is already there and never disturb a
summary.
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR lists what would be summarized and writes nothing.
\fB\-\-status\fR shows the schedule, what is on record, and what is left.
\fB\-\-install\fR schedules a daily run at 05:00 through launchd (macOS only);
\fB\-\-uninstall\fR removes it, leaving the digests in place.
Requires \fBcodex\fR and \fBpython3\fR on \fBPATH\fR; \fB\-\-metrics\-only\fR
needs only \fBpython3\fR and \fB\-\-tokens\-only\fR needs neither.
.TP
.B claudeme cost \fR[\fB\-\-at\fR \fIDATE\fR] [\fB\-\-actual\fR] [\fB\-\-provider\fR \fIP\fR] [\fB\-\-by\fR \fIDIM\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Value the stored token ledgers. Tokens are kept as counted facts and prices as
a dated series, so cost is derived at query time as the sum over days of
tokens times the price in effect that day — correcting a price re-values
history instead of contradicting a stored number.
No transcript is read; the whole command is a scan of what is already on disk.
\fB\-\-actual\fR (the default) values each day at its own prices;
\fB\-\-at\fR values everything at one date's, which is how "what would this
year cost at today's prices" is asked.
\fB\-\-provider\fR is \fBanthropic\fR, \fBopenai\fR or \fBall\fR;
\fB\-\-by\fR is \fBmodel\fR, \fBproject\fR, \fBday\fR or \fBprovider\fR.
Every total states its own coverage: sessions with no ledger, Codex sessions
that predate usage reporting, and models the table deliberately does not price
are reported rather than silently counted as zero.
This is a modelled upper bound on subscription usage, not an invoice.
The OpenAI table is provisional and the output says so.
.TP
.B claudeme snapshot \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-install\fR] [\fB\-\-uninstall\fR] [\fB\-\-status\fR]
Extract per-day usage from every transcript still on disk and upsert it into
.IR ~/.config/claudeme/shared/usage\-history.json .
Nothing is copied or moved: only the aggregates are kept, a few kilobytes a day,
so the numbers survive the deletion of the transcripts they came from. Each day
records its total plus the same total split by model, project and skill.
Cache-creation tokens are recorded split into their 1h and 5m halves, which
bill at different multipliers; days recorded before that split, and whose
transcripts are gone, can only ever be re-priced approximately.
Each run also writes the price table in effect that day to
.IR ~/.config/claudeme/shared/prices/<date>.json ,
an audit record independent of this binary.
Re-running is idempotent — transcripts are append-only, so a full rescan plus a
per-day upsert cannot double count. A recorded day is overwritten only when the
rescan finds at least as much spend; a day that now reads lower has lost
transcripts to retention, and the recorded value is kept and reported.
\fB\-\-install\fR schedules runs at 03:00 and 15:00 through launchd (macOS only).
.TP
.B claudeme history \fR[\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-by\fR \fIdimension\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Print the recorded time series: cost, calls and sessions per day for the last
\fIN\fR days (default 30), or all of it with \fB\-\-all\fR. \fB\-\-by\fR sums the
range by \fBmodel\fR, \fBproject\fR or \fBskill\fR instead of listing days.
\fB\-\-json\fR emits the raw records. Reads only the snapshot file, so it is
instant and covers days whose transcripts are long gone. Sessions are counted per
day, so one running over midnight counts on both; costs are not double counted.
.PP
Costs use public list pricing; on a Max or Pro subscription the real invoice is
zero. Records are deduped by request id, because resuming a session replays
earlier turns into the new transcript. Every report prints the date range it
covers, which is bounded by the transcripts still on disk.
.SH TUI KEYBINDINGS
.TP
.B Up/Down
Navigate the account list.
.TP
.B Enter
Select the highlighted account.
.TP
.B d\fR, \fBx
Delete the highlighted account (with confirmation).
.TP
.B /
Filter/search accounts.
.TP
.B q\fR, \fBEsc\fR, \fBCtrl+C
Quit without making changes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Set by the shell hook to point to the active profile directory. Claude Code reads
all configuration and credentials from this directory instead of \fB~/.claude/\fR.
.TP
.B CLAUDEME_PROFILE
Pins one shell to an account, overriding the active profile and disabling
auto\-switching in that shell. Set by \fBclaudeme pin\fR, or exported by hand.
Accepts an alias or an email.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_PROFILE
Set by the shell hook to the alias (or email) in effect, for use in a prompt or
status line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/claudeme/profiles.json
Account metadata and active account email.
.TP
.I ~/.config/claudeme/aliases.json
Alias-to-email mappings.
.TP
.I ~/.config/claudeme/rules.json
Auto-switch rules (directory pattern to account mappings).
.TP
.I ~/.config/claudeme/settings.json
User preferences (auto_apply on/off).
.TP
.I ~/.config/claudeme/folder_profiles.json
Directory\-to\-account mappings, which take priority over rules.
.TP
.I ~/.config/claudeme/accounts/<email>/
CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR for an account (replaces ~/.claude/). Holds the account's own
\fB.claude.json\fR; everything shared is a symlink into \fBshared/\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.config/claudeme/shared/
One copy of the data shared by all accounts: projects, sessions, history,
settings, plugins, and the session digests.
.SH EXAMPLES
Add two accounts:
.RS
.nf
claudeme add          # login with personal, then /exit
claudeme add          # login with work, then /exit
.fi
.RE
.TP
Create aliases:
.RS
.nf
claudeme alias add personal user@gmail.com
claudeme alias add work user@company.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
Switch accounts:
.B claudeme use work
.TP
Run two accounts in two terminals:
.RS
.nf
claudeme as work            # one launch, active profile untouched
claudeme pin personal       # or pin this shell, then run claudeme freely
.fi
.RE
.TP
Auto-switch by directory:
.RS
.nf
claudeme config auto_apply on
claudeme rule add ~/Developer/work work
.fi
.RE
.TP
Install the shell hook:
.B eval "$(claudeme hook)"
